  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi><mn>23</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>nC</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#8211;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>The processes&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;are isochoric in nature. Hence, for these processes&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math>. As a result, we obtain,<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi><mn>23</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>400</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math></p>",
